hadiths[119]{hadithnumber,text}:
"1355","It is narrated from Ibn Umar (may Allah be pleased with them) that the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said: ""No person should sell over the sale of another."
"1356","It is narrated from Abu Huraira (may Allah be pleased with him) that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him) said: ""No person should sell over the sale of another."
"1357","It is narrated from Abu Huraira (may Allah be pleased with him) that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him) said: ""No man should sell over the sale of his brother."
"1358","From another chain, it is narrated from Abu Huraira (may Allah be pleased with him) from the Prophet (ﷺ) in a similar manner (as the previous hadith)."
"1359","It is narrated from Ibn Umar (may Allah be pleased with them) that the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said: ""No townsman should sell for a villager."
"1360","It is narrated from Jabir (may Allah be pleased with him) that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him) said: ""No townsman should sell for a villager; leave the people alone, Allah provides sustenance to some through others."
"1361","It is narrated from Abu Huraira (may Allah be pleased with him) that the Prophet (ﷺ) said: ""Do not meet the caravans ahead (of their arrival)."
"1362","It is narrated from Abu Huraira (may Allah be pleased with him) that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him) forbade (the sale of) Mulamasah and Munabadhah."
"1363","It is narrated from Ibn Umar (may Allah be pleased with them both) that the Prophet (peace be upon him) forbade najsh (artificially inflating prices to deceive)."
"1364","Abu Huraira (may Allah be pleased with him) narrated that the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said: ""Do not artificially raise prices to deceive."
"1365","It is narrated in a similar manner through another chain from Abu Hurairah, may Allah be pleased with him."
"1366","Through another chain of narration, from Abu Huraira (may Allah be pleased with him), a narration similar to the previous hadith is reported from the Prophet (ﷺ)."
"1367","It is narrated from Ibn Umar (may Allah be pleased with them both) that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him) said: ""Whoever sells a date-palm tree to someone after it has been pollinated, then its (current year's) fruit belongs to the seller, unless the buyer stipulates a condition (that he will also take the fruit), then it is permissible."
"1368","It is narrated from Ibn Umar (may Allah be pleased with them both) that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him) said: ""Whoever sells a date-palm tree after it has been pollinated, its fruit belongs to the seller, except if the buyer stipulates (that he will take the fruit as well)."
"1369","It is narrated from Ibn Umar (may Allah be pleased with them) that the Messenger of Allah (peace be upon him) said: ""Whoever sells a slave who possesses wealth, then his wealth belongs to the seller, unless the buyer stipulates a condition, then it belongs to him."
"1370","It is narrated from Ibn Umar (may Allah be pleased with them both) that the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said: ""Each of the buyer and the seller has the option (to cancel the transaction) as long as they have not separated from each other, except in the case of a sale with a stipulated option (i.e., a sale in which the option has already been stipulated beforehand)."" Imam Shafi'i (may Allah have mercy on him) said that Ibn Umar (may Allah be pleased with them both), who heard this from the Prophet (ﷺ), when he would buy something and wanted to finalize the sale, he would separate from his companion and walk a little, then return (thus the sale would be finalized)."
"1371","It is also narrated from Ibn Umar (may Allah be pleased with them both) through another chain of transmission."
"1372","It is narrated from Abdullah bin Umar (may Allah be pleased with them) that the Messenger of Allah (peace be upon him) said: ""The buyer and the seller both have the option (to cancel the transaction) as long as they have not separated from each other, except in the case of a sale with a stipulated option."
"1373","Ibn Umar (may Allah be pleased with them both) narrated that the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said: ""When two parties engage in a sale, each of them has the option (to cancel or confirm the sale) as long as they have not separated, or unless they have chosen the option of finalizing the sale."" Nafi' said that when Ibn Umar (may Allah be pleased with them both) would make a sale and wanted to finalize it, he would walk a little away from that place and then return."
"1374","Hakim bin Hizam (may Allah be pleased with him) narrated that the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said: ""The buyer and the seller have the option (to cancel the transaction) as long as they have not separated. If they both speak the truth and do not conceal any defect, then blessing is decreed for their transaction. But if they lie and conceal defects, the blessing is erased from their transaction."
"1375","Abu Al-Wadi narrated that we were in a battle when one of our companions sold a horse to a man. When we intended to depart, he brought the dispute to Abu Barzah (may Allah be pleased with him). So Abu Barzah (may Allah be pleased with him) said: I heard the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) say, ""The two parties involved in a sale have the option (to cancel or confirm the sale) until they separate."
"1376","Abdullah bin Tawus narrates from his father that he said: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ gave a man the option after a sale (had become effective), so that man said: May Allah grant you a long life, who are you? The Messenger of Allah ﷺ replied: ""A man from the Quraysh."" Abdullah said: My father used to swear that there is an option after a sale (has become effective)."
"1377","Mukhlad bin Khafaf narrated that I bought a slave, then benefited from him, then found a defect in him. So I filed a case about it with Umar bin Abdul Aziz (may Allah have mercy on him), and he ruled that I should return the slave and also give back the grain (profit) earned from him. Then I went to Urwah (may Allah have mercy on him), and when I told him the matter, he said: I will go to him (Umar) in the evening and inform him that Aisha (may Allah be pleased with her) told me that the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) ruled in such a case that the income belongs to the one who is liable and responsible for bearing the loss. I quickly went to Umar bin Abdul Aziz (may Allah have mercy on him) and told him what Urwah had narrated from Aisha (may Allah be pleased with her) from the Prophet (ﷺ). So Umar (may Allah have mercy on him) said: How easy it is for me to change my decision. Allah knows that I intended nothing but the truth in this matter, but now the way of the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) has reached me in this matter, so I revoke Umar’s decision and implement the Sunnah of the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ). Then in the evening Urwah (may Allah have mercy on him) also came to him, and he ruled for me that I should take back that income."
"1378","It is narrated from Aisha (may Allah be pleased with her) that the Messenger of Allah (peace be upon him) ruled that revenue (income) is due to liability (guarantee)."
"1379","Narrated Aisha (may Allah be pleased with her): The Prophet (ﷺ) said: ""The benefit is for the one who bears the liability of loss."
"1380","It is narrated from Abu Salamah that Abdur Rahman bin Awf (may Allah be pleased with him) bought a slave woman from Asim bin Adi (may Allah be pleased with him), and he was informed that she had a husband, so he returned her."
"1381","It is narrated from Abu Huraira (may Allah be pleased with him) that the Prophet (peace be upon him) said: ""Do not withhold the milk of camels and goats, and if someone buys an animal whose milk has been withheld, then after milking it, he has the option: if he likes it, he may keep it, and if he dislikes it, he may return it along with one sa’ of dates."
"1382","It is narrated from Abu Huraira (may Allah be pleased with him) that he stated: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him) said: ""Do not withhold the milk of camels and goats, and whoever buys an animal whose milk has been withheld, he has the option, after milking it, if he likes it he may keep it, and if he dislikes it he may return it along with one sa’ of dates."
"1383","From another chain, it is narrated from Abu Hurairah, may Allah be pleased with him, from the Prophet ﷺ, similar to the previous hadith. However, in this version, these words are present: He said, ""Return it with a sa' of dates, not with wheat."
"1384","It is narrated from Abu Sa'id Al-Khudri (may Allah be pleased with him) that the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said: ""Do not sell gold for gold except equal for equal, and do not increase one over the other; and do not sell silver for silver except equal for equal, hand to hand, and do not increase one over the other; and do not sell something absent for something present among them."
"1385","It is narrated from Abu Sa'id Al-Khudri (may Allah be pleased with him) that the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said: ""Do not sell gold for gold except in equal measure, and do not sell what is absent for what is present."
"1386","It is narrated from Uthman (may Allah be pleased with him) that he stated: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him) said: ""Do not sell one dinar for two dinars, nor one dirham for two dirhams."
"1387","It is narrated from Abu Huraira (may Allah be pleased with him) that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him) said: ""It is not permissible to have an excess in exchange of dinar for dinar and dirham for dirham."
"1388","It is narrated from Nafi‘ that Ibn ‘Umar (may Allah be pleased with them) said: Do not sell gold for gold except equal for equal, and do not make one more than the other; (similarly) do not sell silver for silver except equal for equal, and do not make one more than the other."
"1389","It is narrated from Mujahid that Ibn Umar (may Allah be pleased with him) said: Dinar is for dinar and dirham is for dirham; any increase in them is not permissible. This is the covenant of our Prophet (ﷺ) with us and our covenant with you."
"1390","It is narrated from Ata bin Yasar (may Allah have mercy on him) that Muawiyah bin Abi Sufyan (may Allah be pleased with him) once sold a gold or silver vessel for more than its weight. So Abu Darda (may Allah be pleased with him) said to him, ""I heard the Prophet (ﷺ) forbidding this."" Muawiyah (may Allah be pleased with him) said, ""In my opinion, there is nothing wrong with it."" So Abu Darda (may Allah be pleased with him) said, ""Who will excuse me regarding Muawiyah? I narrate to him the hadith of the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ), and he tells me his own opinion. I will not stay in the land in which (O Muawiyah!) you reside."
"1391","It is narrated from Malik bin Aws bin Hadthan that he needed to exchange 100 dinars (for dirhams). He stated: Talha bin Ubaidullah (may Allah be pleased with him) called me, and we discussed our matter until it was settled. He took the dinars and began turning them over in his hand, then said: ""Let my male or female treasurer come from Ghaba."" Imam Shafi’i (may Allah have mercy on him) said: I am doubtful about this. And Umar (may Allah be pleased with him) was listening to these things. He (may Allah be pleased with him) said: ""By Allah! Do not separate from Talha until you take the money from him."" Then he said: The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said: ""Gold for gold, if not hand to hand, is usury; dates for dates, if not hand to hand, is usury; and barley for barley, if not hand to hand, becomes usury."" Imam Shafi’i (may Allah have mercy on him) said: I read this hadith to Malik (may Allah have mercy on him) correctly without doubt, then a long time passed and I could not remember, so I became doubtful about 'khazini' and 'khazinatihi'. However, other narrators besides me narrate 'khazini' from Imam Malik (may Allah have mercy on him)."
"1392","From another chain, it is narrated from Umar (may Allah be pleased with him) from the Prophet (ﷺ) similar to the hadith with Malik’s chain, and he said: “Until my treasurer comes.” Imam Shafi’i (may Allah have mercy on him) said, I remembered it without any doubt."
"1393","It is narrated from Umar bin Khattab (may Allah be pleased with him) that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him) said: ""The sale of gold for silver, if not done on the spot, is usury; the sale of grain for grain, if not done on the spot, is usury; the sale of dates for dates, if not done on the spot, is usury; and the sale of barley for barley, if not done on the spot, is usury."
"1394","It is narrated from ‘Ubadah bin Samit (may Allah be pleased with him) that the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said: “Gold for gold, silver for silver, grain for grain, barley for barley, and salt for salt—equal for equal, specified for specified, and do not sell cash for anything but cash. But sell gold for silver, silver for gold, wheat for barley, barley for wheat, dates for salt, and salt for dates as you wish, hand to hand.” He said: “One of Muslim bin Yasar and the other man reduced the dates or the salt.” Abu al-‘Abbas said: In my book it is ‘from Ayyub from Ibn Sirin’, then I saw in the book of the shaykh, that is, Rabi’."
"1395","It is narrated from ‘Ubadah bin Samit (may Allah be pleased with him) that the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said: “Do not sell gold for gold, silver for silver, wheat for wheat, barley for barley, dates for dates, or salt for salt except equal for equal, same for same, and hand to hand. But sell gold for silver, silver for gold, wheat for barley, barley for wheat, dates for salt, and salt for dates as you wish, provided it is hand to hand.” One of them reduced the amount of dates or salt, and one of them added: “Whoever gives more or asks for more has engaged in usury.”"
"1396","Ibn Abbas (may Allah be pleased with them both) narrates that Usama bin Zaid (may Allah be pleased with him) informed me that the Prophet (ﷺ) said: ""Riba (usury) occurs only in the case of credit."
"1397","It is narrated from Ibn Umar (may Allah be pleased with them both) that the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said: ""Whoever buys grain should not sell it until he has taken full possession of it."
"1398","It is narrated from Ibn Umar (may Allah be pleased with them both) that the Prophet (ﷺ) said: ""Whoever buys grain should not sell it until he has taken full possession of it."
"1399","Ibn Abbas (may Allah be pleased with him) narrated that the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) forbade selling grain until it is possessed. And Ibn Abbas (may Allah be pleased with him) said, in his own opinion, I think this rule applies to everything."
"1400","Hakim bin Hizam (may Allah be pleased with him) narrated that the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) forbade me from selling that which is not in my possession."
"1401","Hakim bin Hizam (may Allah be pleased with him) said that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him) said: ""Did I not find out (or has it not reached me) that you sell grain?"" Hakim (may Allah be pleased with him) said, ""Yes, O Messenger of Allah."" Then the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him) said: ""Do not sell the grain at all until you have bought it and become its owner."
"1402","It is narrated from the Prophet ﷺ through another chain from Hakim bin Hizam (may Allah be pleased with him)."
"1403","It is narrated from Ibn Abbas (may Allah be pleased with them) that he said: What the Messenger of Allah (peace be upon him) forbade is the sale of grain which is not taken into possession. Ibn Abbas (may Allah be pleased with them) said from his own opinion, I consider this rule to apply to everything."
"1404","Abu Ayyash Zaid narrated that he asked Sa’d bin Abi Waqqas (may Allah be pleased with him) about selling barley in exchange for wheat. Sa’d (may Allah be pleased with him) asked him, “Which of the two is better?” He replied, “Wheat.” Then Sa’d forbade him and said, “I heard the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) being asked about selling dry dates for fresh dates. The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said: ‘Do fresh dates decrease after they become dry?’ The Companions (may Allah be pleased with them) said, ‘Yes.’ So he (ﷺ) forbade that.”"
"1405","It is narrated from Ibn Umar (may Allah be pleased with him) that the Prophet (peace be upon him) forbade the sale of date palm fruits until their ripeness became apparent, and likewise forbade the sale of dried dates in exchange for dried dates. Abdullah narrated that Zaid bin Thabit (may Allah be pleased with him) informed us that the Prophet (peace be upon him) granted concession in the case of ‘Araya."
"1406","Ismail al-Shaybani or someone else narrated that I sold dates on the trees for one hundred wasq on the condition that if there were more, they would belong to the buyer, and if there were less (than one hundred wasq), the loss would also be on him. Then I asked Ibn Umar (may Allah be pleased with them) and he said that the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) forbade doing so, except that he (ﷺ) permitted it in the sale of ‘ariyya."
"1407","Abdullah bin Umar (may Allah be pleased with them both) narrated from Zaid bin Thabit (may Allah be pleased with him) that the Messenger of Allah (peace be upon him) granted concession to the owner of ‘Ariyyah to sell dates by estimation."
"1408","It is narrated from Abu Huraira (may Allah be pleased with him) that the Prophet (peace be upon him) permitted the sale of ‘Ariyyah for five wasqs or less than five wasqs. Dawud (may Allah have mercy on him) had a doubt, so he said: ""Five wasqs or less than five wasqs."
"1409","Sahl bin Abi Hathmah (may Allah be pleased with him) narrates that the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) forbade selling dried dates for dried dates, but he permitted the sale of ‘Ariyyah, that they may be sold by estimation so that the owners of ‘Ariyyah may eat fresh dates in exchange for them."
"1410","It is narrated from Jabir (may Allah be pleased with him) that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him) forbade the sale of Muzabana, and Muzabana is that the dates on the tree are sold in exchange for dried dates. However, he (peace and blessings be upon him) permitted it in the case of 'Ariya."
"1411","It is narrated from Ibn Umar (may Allah be pleased with them both) that the Messenger of Allah (peace be upon him) forbade Muzabana, and Muzabana is that dates on the tree are sold by measure in exchange for plucked dates, and grapes on the vine are sold by measure in exchange for dried grapes."
"1412","It is narrated from Abu Sa'id Khudri (may Allah be pleased with him) or Abu Hurairah (may Allah be pleased with him) that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him) forbade the sale of Muzabanah and Muhaqalah. Muzabanah is to buy the dates on the tree in exchange for plucked dates, and Muhaqalah is to rent land in exchange for wheat."
"1413","It is narrated from Sa'id bin al-Musayyib (may Allah have mercy on him) that the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) forbade muzabanah and muhaqalah. Muzabanah is the name for buying the dates on a tree in exchange for dried dates, and muhaqalah is the name for buying wheat in its ear in exchange for cleaned wheat. Ibn Shihab (may Allah have mercy on him) said: I inquired about leasing land in exchange for dirhams and dinars, so Ibn al-Musayyib (may Allah have mercy on him) said: There is no harm in that."
"1414","It is narrated from Jabir (may Allah be pleased with him) that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him) forbade mukhabarah, muhaqalah, and muzabanah. Muhaqalah is when a person sells wheat that is still in the ear for one hundred sa’ (a measure) of cleaned wheat. Muzabanah is when dates on the date-palm trees are sold for one hundred sa’ of dried dates. Mukhabarah is when land is given on sharecropping for a third or a fourth of the produce."
"1415","Ibn Umar (may Allah be pleased with them both) narrated that we used to practice mukhabarah and did not see any harm in it, until Rafi‘ (may Allah be pleased with him) expressed the opinion that the Prophet (ﷺ) had forbidden it, so we abandoned it for that reason."
"1416","It is narrated from Ibn Umar (may Allah be pleased with him) that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him) forbade selling fruits before they have ripened."
"1417","It is narrated from Ibn Umar (may Allah be pleased with him) that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him) forbade selling fruits before they ripen. He (peace be upon him) forbade both the seller and the buyer."
"1418","Through another chain, from Ibn Umar (may Allah be pleased with him), the statement of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him) is narrated similar to the previous hadith."
"1419","It is narrated from Anas bin Malik (may Allah be pleased with him) that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him) forbade selling fruits before they reach “zuhū.” So it was asked, O Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him)! What is zuhū? He replied: “Until they become red.” Then the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him) said: “What do you think, if a calamity from the sky befalls the fruits by Allah’s command, then for what reason would you take your brother’s wealth?”"
"1420","It is narrated from Anas bin Malik (may Allah be pleased with him) that the Prophet (ﷺ) forbade the sale of dates on the date palm before they reach the stage of ""zahu."" He was asked, ""What is zahu?"" He replied: ""It is when they become red."
"1421","It is narrated from Umrah that the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) forbade the selling of fruits until they are safe from calamity."
"1422","It is narrated from Abdullah bin Umar, may Allah be pleased with him, that the Prophet ﷺ forbade selling fruits until the fear of calamity has passed. Uthman said, I asked Abdullah, may Allah be pleased with him, what is that time? He replied, when ripeness becomes apparent."
"1423","It is narrated from Jabir (may Allah be pleased with him) that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him) forbade selling fruits before they are ripe. Ibn Jurayj says: I asked whether Jabir (may Allah be pleased with him) specified the date-palm tree or the dates themselves, so he said: Rather, the date-palm tree, and we consider this principle to apply to every fruit."
"1424","It is narrated from Tawus that he heard Ibn Umar (may Allah be pleased with him) say that fruits should not be sold before they are ripe. And we heard Ibn Abbas (may Allah be pleased with him) say that fruits should not be sold until they are eaten (i.e., until they become fit for eating)."
"1425","It is narrated from Abu Ma'bad that Ibn Abbas (may Allah be pleased with him) used to buy dates from his slave before they were eaten, and he did not consider it usury between himself and his slave."
"1426","It is narrated from Jabir bin Abdullah (may Allah be pleased with them both) that the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) forbade selling the fruit of trees for several years, and in the case of a calamity from the sky (which causes loss to the buyers of the fruit), he ordered that compensation be given for it. Imam Shafi’i (may Allah have mercy on him) said: “I heard Sufyan (may Allah have mercy on him) narrate this hadith many times; during the long period I stayed with him, he narrated this hadith so many times that I cannot count, and most of the time when I heard him narrate it, he did not mention ‘he ordered compensation for calamities’ (امر بوضع الجوائح), rather, he did not add anything to the Prophet’s (ﷺ) words ‘he forbade selling for years’ (نہی عن بیع السنین). Then later, he narrated ‘he ordered compensation for calamities.’” So Sufyan (may Allah have mercy on him) said: “Humaid used to mention something after the words ‘selling for years’ and before ‘compensation for calamities’ which I could not remember, and for this reason I refrained from mentioning the words ‘compensation for calamities,’ because I did not know what that thing was. And in this hadith, it is stated that in the case of a calamity from the sky, compensation should be given to the buyers for their loss.”"
"1427","From another chain, a narration similar to the previous hadith is reported from the Prophet ﷺ through Jabir (may Allah be pleased with him)."
"1428","Umrah narrates that in the time of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, a man bought the dates of a garden. He took care of them until he suffered a loss, so he asked the owner of the garden to give him a concession, but the owner swore that he would not give any concession. Then the buyer went to the Messenger of Allah ﷺ and related the matter to him. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: ""Did he swear an oath not to do a good deed?"" When the owner of the garden heard this, he came to the Messenger of Allah ﷺ and said: ""O Messenger of Allah! It is for him."" (i.e., I have given the concession.)"
"1429","It is narrated from Jabir (may Allah be pleased with him) that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him) forbade selling the fruit of the tree for several years."
"1430","It is also narrated through another chain from Jabir (may Allah be pleased with him) from the Prophet (ﷺ) similar to the previous hadith."
"1431","It is narrated from Jabir (may Allah be pleased with him): He said, I forbade Ibn Zubair (may Allah be pleased with them both) from selling date palm trees for several years."
"1432","Jabir bin Abdullah (may Allah be pleased with them) narrates that I forbade Ibn Zubair (may Allah be pleased with them) from selling date palm trees for several years in advance."
"1433","It is narrated from Jabir bin Abdullah (may Allah be pleased with him) that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him) forbade selling for several years."
"1434","Through another chain as well, a narration similar to the previous hadith is reported from the Prophet ﷺ through Jabir (may Allah be pleased with him)."
"1435","It is narrated from Ikrimah (may Allah have mercy on him) that Sayyiduna Ibn Abbas (may Allah be pleased with them both) states: Do not buy and sell on credit until the salary (i.e., wages) is received, until the crop has been harvested, and until the grain has been sold."
"1436","Jabir bin Abdullah (may Allah be pleased with them both) narrates that the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) forbade selling a heap of dates of unknown weight in exchange for dates whose weight is known."
"1437","It is narrated from Ibn Abbas (may Allah be pleased with him), he said: ""I bear witness that Allah Almighty has declared the sale of Salam (advance payment for deferred delivery) for a fixed term permissible in His Book and has also granted permission for it."" Then he recited this verse: ""O you who believe! When you contract a debt for a fixed term..."" (__QUR_N__: Al-Baqarah: 282)"
"1438","It is narrated from Ibn Abbas (may Allah be pleased with him) that when the Prophet (ﷺ) came to Madinah, people used to conduct Salam transactions in dates for one or two years, and sometimes the word three was also mentioned. So the Prophet (ﷺ) said: ""Whoever does a Salam sale should do so with a specified measure and specified weight, for a specified period."" Imam Shafi’i (may Allah have mercy on him) said: ""Just as I have narrated the hadith, I memorized it after hearing it several times from Sufyan."
"1439","He told me, whom I trust, he narrated from Sufyan, and he said just as I have said, and regarding the period he said: ""That the period should be known."
"1440","It is narrated from Ibn Abbas (may Allah be pleased with him), he said: ""When the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) came to Madinah, the people of Madinah used to conduct Salam sales in dates for one, two, or three years. Then the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said: 'Whoever does a Salam sale, let him do so for a specified measure, a specified weight, and a specified term.'"
"1441","It is narrated from Nafi‘ that it is reported from Ibn ‘Umar (may Allah be pleased with him) that he did not see any harm in a person selling something for a fixed period of time, even though he did not possess its original at that time."
"1442","It is also narrated in a similar manner from Ibn Umar (may Allah be pleased with him) through another chain of transmission."
"1443","It is narrated from Ibn Abbas (may Allah be pleased with him) that he said: ""It is well known that a man gives a dinar to another and takes grain from him."
"1444","Qasim reports that I heard Abdullah ibn Abbas (may Allah be pleased with him) being asked by a man about a person who made a salam sale in garments and wanted to sell them before becoming their owner. Ibn Abbas (may Allah be pleased with him) said: ""This is like selling silver for silver,"" and he disliked this sale. Imam Malik (may Allah have mercy on him) said: ""In our opinion, since he wanted to sell it at a higher price to the same person with whom he made the sale, (that is why it was disliked), and if he sells it to someone other than the one from whom he bought the goods, then there is no harm in this sale."
"1445","Ibn Abbas (may Allah be pleased with him) said: ""We do not consider it objectionable to sell silver for silver in a spot transaction (bay' salam)."
"1446","It is narrated from Amr bin Dinar (may Allah have mercy on him) that Ibn Umar (may Allah be pleased with him) considered it permissible."
"1447","It is narrated from Abu Rafi’ (may Allah be pleased with him) that the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) borrowed a camel from a man. Later, camels for zakat came to the Prophet (ﷺ), so he (ﷺ) ordered me to return the man's camel to him."
"1448","The freed slave of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, Abu Rafi’ (may Allah be pleased with him), narrated that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ borrowed a camel. Then camels for zakat came to him ﷺ. Abu Rafi’ said: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ ordered me to return the man's camel. When I said: O Messenger of Allah! I can only find an excellent four-year-old camel, the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: “Give it to him; the best among people is the one who is best in repaying debts.”"
"1449","A narration with the same meaning as the previous hadith is reported from Abu Hurairah (may Allah be pleased with him)."
"1450","Jabir (may Allah be pleased with him) narrated that a slave pledged allegiance to the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) for migration, and the Prophet (ﷺ) did not know that he was a slave until his owner came to take him back. So the Prophet (ﷺ) said: ""Sell him."" Thus, the Prophet (ﷺ) bought him in exchange for two Abyssinian slaves. After that, the Prophet (ﷺ) did not take allegiance from anyone until he asked whether he was a slave or free."
"1451","Ziyad bin Abi Tamim (the freed slave of Uthman bin Affan, may Allah be pleased with him) narrated that the Prophet ﷺ sent a companion to collect charity, and he brought back young camels suitable for riding. When the Prophet ﷺ saw them, he said: ""You have destroyed yourself and (caused) others to perish as well."" Upon this, he said: O Messenger of Allah! I used to sell two or three camels for one young riding camel in cash, and I knew of the Prophet’s ﷺ need for riding camels. Then the Prophet ﷺ said: ""Then that is fine."
"1452","It is narrated from Ibn Abbas (may Allah be pleased with them) that he was asked about exchanging one camel for two camels, so he said: Sometimes one camel is better than two camels."
"1453","It is narrated from Ali bin Abi Talib (may Allah be pleased with him) that he sold a camel named 'Usayfeer' for twenty camels for a specified period."
"1454","It is narrated from Nafi‘ that Ibn ‘Umar (may Allah be pleased with them both) bought a riding animal in exchange for four camels, with the condition that he would deliver them at the place of Rabadhah."
"1455","With this same chain, it is narrated from Ibn Umar (may Allah be pleased with them both) that he bought a camel in exchange for four camels, with the condition that they would be given at the place of Rabadhah."
"1456","Qasim bin Abu Bazah narrated: When I came to Madinah, I found that camels had been slaughtered, and their meat had been divided into portions. Each portion was equal to a one-year-old kid of a goat or sheep. I wanted to buy one of these portions, but a man from among the people of Madinah said to me that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ had forbidden selling a living animal in exchange for a dead one. Ibn Jurayj said: I inquired about this man, and I was given a good introduction about him."
"1457","It is narrated from Abu Bakr Siddiq (may Allah be pleased with him) through Ibn Abbas (may Allah be pleased with them both) that he disliked selling an animal in exchange for meat."
"1458","It is narrated from Ibn Abbas (may Allah be pleased with them) that he disliked the sale of wool on sheep and the sale of milk in the udders of goats without measuring."
"1459","It is narrated from ‘Urwah bin Ja‘d (may Allah be pleased with him) that the Prophet ﷺ gave him a dinar so that he could buy a goat for him. He bought two goats with that dinar, then sold one goat for a dinar and returned the dinar as well as a goat to the Prophet ﷺ. So the Messenger of Allah ﷺ prayed for blessing in his trade, and after that, his situation was such that if he bought even dust, he would profit from it."
"1460","The same previous incident or its equivalent meaning is also narrated through another chain of transmission."
"1461","Ibn Wahla Al-Misri narrates that he asked Ibn Abbas (may Allah be pleased with him) about wine extracted from grapes. Ibn Abbas (may Allah be pleased with him) said: “A man gifted a wineskin of wine to the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ), so the Prophet (ﷺ) said: ‘Do you not know that Allah Almighty has made it unlawful?’ The man said: ‘No.’ Then he whispered to a man sitting beside him. The Prophet (ﷺ) said: ‘What did you whisper to him?’ He said: ‘I told him to sell it.’ Upon hearing this, the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said: ‘He who made its drinking unlawful has also made its selling unlawful.’ Then he opened both wineskins until they were empty (i.e., the wine was poured out and wasted).”"
"1462","Ibn Abbas (may Allah be pleased with them both) narrated that Umar (may Allah be pleased with him) learned that a man had sold wine, so he said: May Allah destroy him! Does he not know that the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said: May Allah destroy the Jews, for fat was made unlawful for them, so they melted it and sold it."
"1463","It is narrated from Ibn Umar (may Allah be pleased with them both) that some people from Iraq said, ""We will buy dates and grapes, then extract wine from them and sell it."" So Abdullah bin Umar (may Allah be pleased with them both) said: ""I call Allah, His angels, and whoever among humans and jinn is listening as witnesses over you that I do not command you to sell it, nor to buy it, nor to extract it, nor to serve it, because it is filth and a satanic act."
"1464","It is narrated from Abu Mas'ud Ansari (may Allah be pleased with him) that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him) forbade taking the price of a dog, the wages of a prostitute, and the offering given to a soothsayer."
"1465","It is narrated from Abu Mas'ud Ansari (may Allah be pleased with him) that the Prophet (peace and blessings be upon him) forbade taking the price of a dog, the wages of a prostitute, and the offering given to a soothsayer. Imam Malik (may Allah have mercy on him) said: ""Indeed, the sale of both hunting and non-hunting dogs has been deemed disliked because the Prophet (peace and blessings be upon him) forbade the price of a dog."
"1466","It is narrated from Hanzalah bin Qais that he asked Rafi' bin Khadij (may Allah be pleased with him) about leasing land. He replied that the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) forbade leasing land. Then he asked, ""Is it forbidden if it is for gold and silver?"" He replied, ""If it is for gold and silver, then there is no harm in it."
"1467","It is narrated from Sa'id bin al-Musayyib, may Allah have mercy on him, that he was asked about leasing land in exchange for gold and silver, so he said: ""There is no harm in it."
"1468","A narration similar to the previous hadith is reported from Hisham bin Urwah, may Allah have mercy on him, from his father."
"1469","It is also narrated in the same manner from Salim (may Allah have mercy on him)."
"1470","It is narrated from Ibn Umar (may Allah be pleased with them both) that when someone would lease his land, he would stipulate the condition that he (the lessee) would not put manure (in the land), and this was before Abdullah bin Umar (may Allah be pleased with them both) stopped leasing out his land."
"1471","It is narrated from Sa'id bin al-Musayyib, may Allah have mercy on him, that when the Messenger of Allah ﷺ conquered Khaybar, he said to the Jews: ""I allow you to remain here on the condition upon which Allah has settled you, and that is that the fruits will be divided equally between us and you."" Then the Messenger of Allah ﷺ would send Ibn Rawahah, may Allah be pleased with him, and he would estimate the fruits and divide them, then he would say to them: ""The choice of selection is yours."
"1472","It is narrated from Sa'id bin al-Musayyib, may Allah have mercy on him, that when the Messenger of Allah ﷺ conquered Khaybar, he said to the Jews: ""I allow you to stay here on the condition upon which Allah has settled you, that is, the dates will be divided equally between us and you."" He said, then the Messenger of Allah ﷺ would send Abdullah bin Rawahah, may Allah be pleased with him, and he would estimate for them and say that the choice of selection is yours, so they would take first."
"1473","It is narrated from Sulaiman bin Yasar (may Allah have mercy on him) that the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) used to send Abdullah bin Rawahah (may Allah be pleased with him), and he would make estimations and divide between you and the Jews."
